---
/**
 * The platform sprite (S026, D294): every sign-up mark's path data, painted once per page.
 *
 * Why a sprite and not three inline `<svg>`s: a `/schedule` page prints one cluster per open date, and each
 * cluster carries one link per platform. Inlining the glyphs would ship the Meetup path (2,356 characters @measured
 * 2026-11-13 by `curl -sL https://cdn.simpleicons.org/meetup | wc -c`) once per link — nine links on a nine-row
 * page, nine copies of the same drawing. A `<symbol>` in a hidden sprite plus a `<use href="#pm-meetup">` in
 * each link ships the path data once and the references cost ~22 bytes each.
 *
 * The trade is measured, not assumed (`node design/mark-cost.mjs`, @measured 2026-11-13, Linux native, Node
 * 24.22.1, gzip bytes of the built page): the sprite costs **+2,195 gzip** on `/` (1 cluster) and **+2,207** on
 * `/schedule` (5 clusters), while inlining the three glyphs into those same pages costs **+2,118** and **+2,305**.
 * So a one-cluster page pays **77 gzip** more for the sprite, and a five-cluster page saves **98**. The probe
 * repeats one cluster 1-8 times to find the crossover: sprite wins at **3 clusters** (2,488 vs 2,522 gzip on a
 * homepage-shaped page; 2,545 vs 2,566 on a schedule-shaped one) and loses at 2 (2,462 vs 2,448) — an earlier
 * "breaks even at two clusters" figure divided a gzip-deduplicated total and is deleted, not restated (D300).
 * Two consequences the gates enforce: the sprite is emitted by
 * `SignUpLinks.astro` for the page that paints a cluster, never by the layout, so a page painting no mark ships
 * no glyph bytes (the counterfactual: `/chat` would pay **+2,201 gzip** for drawings it never paints — 41.0% of
 * that page's 5,372 gzip bytes); and the sprite is printed once per page render, because two sprites on one page
 * is two sets of the same ids.
 *
 * `class="hidden"` is load-bearing in the boring sense: a sprite with no symbols painted is still a document
 * node, and an `<svg>` with no width would add nothing to the layout anyway — but a *display:none* element is
 * the one thing that provably cannot overlap anything, which is why the sprite is not given a size. The
 * `<use>` references resolve regardless of the sprite's own visibility; that is how the technique works.
 *
 * `SignUpLinks.astro` renders it for the render that claims a cluster (`spriteFor` claims one sprite per
 * `Astro.locals`, the request-scoped object, and deliberately **not** on `Astro.url.pathname`: a claim keyed on
 * the path lives in a module loaded once per process, so the second request for `/` would have painted three
 * `<use>` refs with no symbols behind them (D297, caught by `node design/sprite-claim.mjs` against a running dev
 * server), so a page with no cluster never asks for it — and
 * `design/render-gate.mjs` (rule 4c) checks the symbol is really in the document for every mark a link
 * references, because a broken `href` paints an empty 16px box that looks, to a class scanner, exactly like a
 * mark.
 */
import { MARKS, markId } from '../lib/platform-marks.mjs';
---

<svg class="hidden" aria-hidden="true">
  {MARKS.map((m) => (
    <symbol id={markId(m.platform)} viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <path d={m.d} />
    </symbol>
  ))}
</svg>
